def GlobalDocumentation {
  code Intro =[{..
  -------------------------------------------------------------------
  NOTE: This file is automatically generated by running llvm-tblgen
  -gen-kitsune-inst-attr-docs. Do not edit this file by hand!!
  -------------------------------------------------------------------

====================================
LLVM Instruction Attribute Reference
====================================
.. contents::
  :local:

.. |br| raw:: html

  <br/>

These are Kitsune-specific "attributes" that may be attached to LLVM
instructions. These use the existing instruction attribute mechanism available
for LLVM's instructions. However, these are **not** registered with LLVM, so
they do not have a `kind` that LLVM knows about. They are used exclusively by
Kitsune's passes.

Kitsune's instruction attributes must be one of the following kinds.

* **Flag Attributes**: These are attributes that do not take a value. Only
  their presence or absence is significant.

* **Valued Attributes**: These are attributes that take a value. Currently,
  only certain types of values are supported. These, together with the
  corresponding type that will appear in the LLVM-IR metadata are
  summarized in the table below.

  =============  ============
  Source type    LLVM-IR type
  =============  ============
  ``int32_t``    ``i32``
  ``int64_t``    ``i64``
  ``MDNode*``    ``MDNode``
  ``Loop*``      ``MDNode``
  ``StringRef``  ``[n x i8]``
  =============  ============

  Here, a source type of ``MDNode`` refers to a raw metadata node constructed by
  the user. It can contain an arbitrary number of operands. The number and types
  of these operands are not known to Kitsune. It is up to the user to use these
  correctly.

  A source type of ``Loop`` will result in the value of the instruction
  attribute being the ID node of a loop. This is used to associate an
  instruction with a loop. The intention is to allow an instruction to be
  "tracker" if it is moved out of a loop.

* **Enum Attributes**: These are a special case of the valued attributes
  where the value type is an **enum** defined in Kitsune's source. The
  type of the value in LLVM-IR metadata will always be ``i32``.

In the example below, the instruction metadata attached to the add instruction
is
:ref:`kit.inst.source.loop <inst-attr-source-loop>`. The value indicates
that it is associated with the loop whose id is `!0`.

.. code :: kitll

  define void @f(i64 %n) {
  entry:
    br label %for.i.header

  for.i.header:
    %inc.i = add i64 %i, 1, !kit.inst.source.loop !1
    %exitcond.i.not = icmp eq i64 %inc.i, %n
    br i1 %exitcond.i.not, label %for.i.exit, label %for.i.header, !llvm.loop !0

  for.i.exit:
    ret void
  }

  !0 = distinct !{!0}
  !1 = !{!0}

Every attribute has an entry in the ``InstAttrKind`` enum.
}];
}

include "kitsune/Core/InstAttrs.td"
